nodecode-athena is a rewrite of athenaforhermes's athenaforhermes/athena:
https://github.com/athenaforhermes/athena
commit 3ef0dbddf18545f2a2d5b2e192dada16e3b0eda8
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 athenaforhermes (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the catalogue read (the store's JSON API, one page asked
for by query, with the API's wrapped and bare rows read the same way, and its
tags read as names), the search answer's fields, install by a shallow git
clone into the apps folder, the launch of an app's own start command in the
background with its output in a log beside it, the stop of the process this
cell started, the log tail, and the reading of a log that names a cause and
the step that answers it. The tool ids are the original's: athena_search,
athena_apps, athena_install, athena_launch, athena_stop, athena_logs,
athena_status. The Python is Common Lisp here, and the seams that run a
program are stubbed in the tests, so the suite runs offline.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes plugin drove its own Node engine
(pinokiod, vendored under engine/) and served a desktop UI of its own with
routes, panels and a background HTTP server; here the verbs are native and the
seam is a program the machine already has (git, sh, node), so no Node runtime
is bundled and no port is opened by the cell. That also means the original's
engine-level features have no counterpart here and are not claimed:
athena_publish (pushing an app to a GitHub repository), athena_files (a
browser over an app's files), athena_machines and athena_space (WSL and QEMU
guests), athena_capabilities and athena_generate (asking an installed app
what it can do, and calling it), athena_schedule and athena_runs (scheduled
app runs), athena_build_app (making a new app from a template), athena_fix
(repairing an app in place), athena_delete, athena_store_detail and
athena_report. The credential hand-off (a credential from the harness's own
secret store, given to an approved app as an environment variable) is left to
the app's own settings here, because a native cell hands an app the
environment it starts it with.
